  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simple workflow showing how single-crystal catalysts are prepared and then used in different catalytic modes. On the left, several synthesis routes are listed—such as molten salt, vapour deposition, hydrothermal and non-hydrothermal methods, sacrificial templates, and combustion—suggesting multiple ways to make these materials. These methods feed into a step labeled “bulk-scale control,” which leads to a single crystal represented by a blue hexagonal particle.\n\nFrom this single-crystal material, arrows branch to three application categories on the right: thermochemical, electrochemical, and photochemical catalysis. Each category is illustrated with a recognizable symbol—a thermometer for heat-driven processes, a lightning bolt for electrically driven reactions, and a sun for light-driven reactions. In all three cases, small blue spheres and circular arrows appear to indicate reacting molecules and catalytic turnover at the crystal surface.\n\nThe overall message is that controlling the synthesis of catalysts at the single-crystal, bulk scale enables their use across several major catalytic platforms, and this structural control is linked to “exceptional catalytic activity.” The figure emphasizes the connection between preparation strategy, well-defined crystal structure, and improved catalytic performance.",
